you were watching ColdFusion TV hi  welcome to another ColdFusion video so  last week I took to Twitter to ask  people what they wanted to see in terms  of shorter format videos one of the  topics that came up was Apple's first  sales decline since 2003 so why are  Apple sales falling in this video we'll  find out last month Apple reported its  first year-over-year drop in iPhone  sales in its history  Apple Inc has posted its first ever  decline in iPhone sales and first a  revenue drop in more than a decade as  the company credited with inventing the  smart phone struggles with an  increasingly saturated markets a 16  percent drop when compared to the first  quarter of 2015 and a 32 percent drop  when compared to the last quarter of  2015  here's a chart breakdown of Apple's  total revenue structure iPhones are so  crucial to Apple's business that this  drop cost them 13% of their entire  revenue despite all of this Apple's  still managed to sell 51 million phones  this quarter second only to Samsung's 81  million before we get into the reasons  for the decline I wanted to get a feel  of what people thought about Apple  currently so I went out and asked a few  of my friends the average answer went  something like this Apple just isn't  innovating like they used to and Tim  Cook just isn't the same breed as Steve  Jobs I totally agree with all of this  and I would add that the smart phone  competition from Android is stronger  than it's ever been  so for Apple to sit still is a little  risky at the moment doesn't look like  there is anything that would drive  significant growth for Apple from here  as they've been behaving now you know if  they came out with a breakthrough  product or price then you might see a  change particularly if it's different  than the other smartphone makers in this  market but absent that kind of  innovation in in price or features I  think we're in a much more gradual and  uncertain  that being said one of my friends did  say something very interesting  this friend told me that he thought  Apple wasn't stupid enough to let the  world just pass them by and that they  were secretly syncing millions of  dollars of R&amp;D to surprise the soil with  a revolutionary product in the next two  to five years and as it turns out he is  not alone of those views  Dustin seemed like it's going to come in  iPads which are in a serious decline it  doesn't seem like it's going to come in  max which also declined so if you want  the kind of growth that Apple investors  are used to they're going to need  something else we've been talking about  a TV for years that seems like it was  kicked to the backburner possibly a car  there's another really interesting  moment on the earnings call where one of  the analysts asked about virtual reality  and Tim Cook is of course isn't going to  reveal anything but he kind of hinted  that yeah this is pretty important so  maybe DR is where they're going to go  and maybe they will use a chunk of that  money to push ahead of it their  competitors as interesting as this  theory maybe it still doesn't help the  fact that Apple styles have fallen so  why is this happening the major reasons  are as follows number one underlying  global deflation I'll try my best to  explain this one deflation is an  economic term that basically means the  reduction of money supply usually  resulting in falling prices deflation is  natural and isn't inherently bad but  according to some economists like Peter  chefs Jim Rickards  David Stockman and Mike Maloney who I  recently just had a chat with by the way  according to these economists central  banks have tried to fight deflation  since the 2008 crash by providing  trillions of dollars worth of cheap  low-interest credit instead of this  credit going to productive use it's  actually been allocated wrongly or put  towards bad investments that don't help  the real economy  this causes market distortions that are  only just now starting to reverse and  economies are just left with too much  private household and corporate debt the  end of a worldwide era in which central  bank's printed money injected liquidity  supported markets manipulated and  intruded in the pricing of financial  assets like never before and what it did  was create a massive credit expansion in  the world that's stopping we had 40  trillion of debt in the world in 1995 we  have 225 trillion today everywhere is at  peak debt just as so much debt  saturation it all has to stop being paid  off because of this there's less  spending overall in the economy and a  deflationary cycle begins so what is  this deflationary cycle there's less  spending throughout the economy which  means job cuts the people who lose their  jobs are going to spend less and this  eventually means less profits for the  companies that rely on consumer spending  which means even more job cuts and it  continues this is a macroeconomic force  and affects all companies not just Apple  just take a look at Intel laying off  12,000 jobs and other companies like HP  IBM and Macy's this is all out of apples  control but is probably the biggest  reason for the decrease in sales in my  opinion the second reason for the fall  of Apple sales is market saturation of  the smartphone segment in Western  markets about 60 to 80 percent of those  who want a smartphone have one and even  China a once large engine of iPhone  growth is seeing market saturation and  slow down overall it's already at a  point where a one or two year old  smartphone is good enough for someone  who just wants to shoot some casual  pictures use social media text and chat  the only real place is remaining for  smartphone market growth are the  emerging markets but this is where  Android is king at the moment Google is  working hard to provide capable  smartphones at an affordable price with  efforts like Android one yes there is  the cheaper $400 iPhone se but this is  still expensive compared to some Android  equivalent in addition to this the $400  iPhone se isn't as it seems this leads  on to the third reason the third reason  is a high exchange rate of the u.s.  dollar versus all major currencies for  example here in Australia just a few  years ago the US dollar and the  Australian dollar are about the same but  and for US dollar is 1.3 six times more  expensive a higher US dollar makes  everything that's exported from the  United States more expensive and less  attractive to overseas buyers again this  is another external force for Apple  so iPhones are going to be more  expensive everywhere except for the  United States so that's it  those are my three top exact reasons why  Apple sales are slowing I guess you  could call it four if you add that lack  of innovation so to recap the main  reasons are a lack of innovation  macroeconomic conditions market  saturation and a high exchange rate all  in all it's certainly not the end of the  world for Apple after all they still  make more money selling physical  products than any company on earth this  may just be a temporary blip Apple and  the iPhone 7 may have impressive sales  again but the global economic headwinds  are going to be strong for Apple and all  companies for that matter what do you  guys think do you think Apple's reign is  over or do you think they still have a  lot of life left in them let me know in  the comments section below I also just  want to thank everyone that's supporting  me on patreon if you want to help  support I'll leave a link in the  description no pressure if you don't  want to you don't have to all right  thanks guys give a thumbs up if you  liked this video and subscribe if you're  new to this channel I'll see you again  soon for the next video cheers guys have  a good one  cold fusion it's rethinking  you
 

